import HeckeTemperleyLieb.MarkovTransfer

/-!
# QOU.IwahoriHecke.MarkovTraceSymmetries — T, P and CPT on the Markov trace

The three discrete-symmetry conjectures of the nuclear-interactions chapter
(`conj:t-from-trace-cyclicity`, `conj:p-from-braid-mirror`, `conj:cpt-d6-d7-d8-closure`)
are statements about the Markov trace `tr_M` on signed braid words. This file settles
the trace-level content of all three, for every number of strands, over any commutative
ring `R` and any Hecke quadratic `sᵢ² = a·sᵢ + b`.

## Setting

`A` is any `R`-algebra carrying a `HeckeFamily N a b s` (quadratic, braid and far
commutation on `s₀, …, s_{N-1}`). A *Markov trace* is an `R`-linear `t : A → R` with
`t 1 = 1`, `t (x y) = t (y x)`, and `t (x · s_k) = z · t x` for `x` generated by
`s₀, …, s_{k-1}`, exactly the hypotheses of `JonesNormalForm.markov_unique`.

A signed letter `(i, e)` is evaluated as `α e • 1 + β e • s i` for arbitrary
`α β : Bool → R`. The usual braid convention, `σᵢ ↦ sᵢ` and `σᵢ⁻¹ ↦ sᵢ − a` (valid when
`b = 1`, which is the corpus convention `σ² = (q − q⁻¹)σ + 1`), is `stdα a`, `stdβ`.

## Results (sorry-free)

* **T (proved, every `N`).** `markov_reverse`: `t (eval w.reverse) = t (eval w)` for every
  signed word `w` and every letter convention. The proof does not use an anti-involution
  on `A`, which need not exist as a map one can write down. It uses the opposite algebra:
  `op ∘ s` is again a Hecke family in `Aᵐᵒᵖ` (the relations are palindromic), `t ∘ unop`
  is a Markov trace there with the same `z`, and evaluating `w` in `Aᵐᵒᵖ` gives
  `op (eval w.reverse)`. Markov transfer (`markov_transfer`, which rests on uniqueness)
  then identifies the two values. This is the anti-automorphism route that
  `QOU.BraidsAndKnots.TFromTraceCyclicity` names and leaves unformalised. Cyclicity alone
  does not give it, as that file correctly says; uniqueness does.

* **P with `q` fixed (refuted).** `mirror_not_trace_preserving`: if `a ≠ 0` and `N ≥ 1`,
  the mirror `σᵢ^ε ↦ σᵢ^{-ε}` does not preserve the Markov trace. The smallest witness is
  the one-letter braid `σ₀ ∈ B₂`: `tr_M(σ₀) = z` and `tr_M(σ₀⁻¹) = z − a`. Over
  `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]` with `a = q − q⁻¹` this is a difference of `q − q⁻¹ ≠ 0`.

* **What the mirror does instead (proved).** `mirror_law`: the mirror carries the Markov
  trace with data `(a, z)` to the Markov trace with data `(−a, z − a)`. Precisely,
  `sᵢ⁻¹ = sᵢ − a` is a Hecke family with quadratic `x² = −a·x + 1` (`heckeFamily_inv`), `t`
  is a Markov trace for it with parameter `z − a` (`markov_inv`), and so
  `t (eval (mirror w))` equals the value on `w` of *any* Markov trace with data
  `(−a, z − a)`, in any algebra. With `a = q − q⁻¹`, `−a` is `a` at `q⁻¹`; so the mirror
  is a change of parameters, `q ↦ q⁻¹` together with `z ↦ z − (q − q⁻¹)`, not a symmetry
  at fixed `q`.

* **CPT (refuted, in the form stated).** In `conj:cpt-d6-d7-d8-closure`, `C` acts on
  colour-sheet flavour labels with no action on Hecke generators, so on a signed braid
  word the composite `C·P·T` is `w ↦ mirror (w.reverse)`, i.e. braid inversion
  `β ↦ β⁻¹`. `cpt_not_trace_preserving`: under the same hypotheses this does not preserve
  the Markov trace, with the same witness `σ₀` (a one-letter word is its own reverse).
  Since `T` *is* trace-preserving (above), the failure is entirely `P`'s.

* **qCPT (proved).** `qcpt_law`: `C·P·T` together with the parameter change
  `(a, z) ↦ (−a, z − a)` (i.e. `q ↦ q⁻¹`, `z ↦ z − (q − q⁻¹)`) preserves the Markov trace.

* **The `z` shift is absorbed by writhe normalisation (proved).** Take a unit `μ` with
  `μ² z = z − a` (`IsNormaliser`), so `μ^{writhe} · tr` is the HOMFLY-type invariant with loop
  value `D = μ z`. Then `μ⁻¹` normalises `(−a, z − a)` with the same `D`
  (`isNormaliser_inv`), and `normalised_mirror_law` / `normalised_qcpt_law` say the
  normalised invariant of `P β` or `CPT β` at `(a, μ)` equals that of `β` at `(−a, μ⁻¹)`.
  So qCPT on the normalised invariant is `q ↦ q⁻¹`, `μ ↦ μ⁻¹`, with no separate `z` move.

## Characters that are not Markov traces

The class `QOU.BraidsAndKnots.TFromTraceCyclicity` quantifies over every matrix
representation `HeckeRep n d` and its *character*, which is cyclic but is not a Markov
trace, so uniqueness does not apply. That stronger statement is proved separately, for every
cyclic functional and every `n`, in `QOU.IwahoriHecke.HeckeTraceReversal`.

-- Ref: [jones1987] Jones, *Hecke algebra representations of braid groups and link
--      polynomials*, Ann. Math. 126 (1987), §4–5.
-- Ref: [geckpfeiffer2000] Geck–Pfeiffer, *Characters of finite Coxeter groups and
--      Iwahori–Hecke algebras*, §8.2.
-/

namespace QOU.IwahoriHecke.MarkovTraceSymmetries

open QOU.IwahoriHecke.JonesNormalForm MulOpposite

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {N : ℕ} {a b : R}

/-! ### Signed words and their evaluation -/

section Words

variable {A : Type*} [Ring A] [Algebra R A]

/-- A signed letter `(i, e)` evaluated as `α e • 1 + β e • s i`. -/
def letter (α β : Bool → R) (s : ℕ → A) (p : Fin N × Bool) : A :=
  α p.2 • (1 : A) + β p.2 • s p.1

/-- A signed word evaluated left to right. -/
def evalWord (α β : Bool → R) (s : ℕ → A) : List (Fin N × Bool) → A
  | [] => 1
  | p :: w => letter α β s p * evalWord α β s w

@[simp] theorem evalWord_nil (α β : Bool → R) (s : ℕ → A) :
    evalWord α β s ([] : List (Fin N × Bool)) = 1 := rfl

@[simp] theorem evalWord_cons (α β : Bool → R) (s : ℕ → A) (p : Fin N × Bool)
    (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    evalWord α β s (p :: w) = letter α β s p * evalWord α β s w := rfl

theorem evalWord_append (α β : Bool → R) (s : ℕ → A) (u v : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    evalWord α β s (u ++ v) = evalWord α β s u * evalWord α β s v := by
  induction u with
  | nil => simp
  | cons p u ih => simp [ih, mul_assoc]

/-- Evaluation commutes with any algebra map that matches the generators. -/
theorem map_evalWord {B : Type*} [Ring B] [Algebra R B] (f : A →ₐ[R] B) (α β : Bool → R)
    (s : ℕ → A) (s' : ℕ → B) (hf : ∀ i, i < N → f (s i) = s' i) (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    f (evalWord α β s w) = evalWord α β s' w := by
  induction w with
  | nil => simp
  | cons p w ih =>
    simp only [evalWord_cons, map_mul, ih, letter, map_add, map_smul, map_one,
      hf p.1 p.1.isLt]

/-- Evaluating in the opposite algebra reverses the word. -/
theorem evalWord_op (α β : Bool → R) (s : ℕ → A) (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    evalWord α β (fun i => op (s i)) w = op (evalWord α β s w.reverse) := by
  induction w with
  | nil => simp
  | cons p w ih =>
    rw [evalWord_cons, ih, List.reverse_cons, evalWord_append, op_mul]
    congr 1
    simp [letter]

/-- The standard braid convention: `σᵢ ↦ sᵢ` and `σᵢ⁻¹ ↦ sᵢ − a` (the inverse when `b = 1`). -/
def stdα (a : R) : Bool → R := fun e => if e then 0 else -a

/-- The standard braid convention, linear part. -/
def stdβ : Bool → R := fun _ => 1

/-- The mirror `σᵢ^ε ↦ σᵢ^{-ε}` on signed words (parity `P`, with `q` held fixed). -/
def mirror (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) : List (Fin N × Bool) :=
  w.map fun p => (p.1, !p.2)

/-- The composite `C·P·T` on signed words. `C` has no action on Hecke generators, so this
is `P ∘ T`, i.e. braid inversion. -/
def cpt (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) : List (Fin N × Bool) :=
  mirror w.reverse

end Words

/-! ### The opposite algebra carries the same Hecke family -/

section Opposite

variable {A : Type*} [Ring A] [Algebra R A] {s : ℕ → A}

/-- The type-`A` relations are palindromic, so `op ∘ s` is a Hecke family in `Aᵐᵒᵖ`. -/
theorem heckeFamily_op (hs : HeckeFamily N a b s) :
    HeckeFamily N a b (fun i => op (s i)) where
  quad i hi := by
    apply unop_injective
    simp [hs.quad i hi]
  braid i hi := by
    apply unop_injective
    simpa [mul_assoc] using hs.braid i hi
  comm i j hij hj := by
    apply unop_injective
    simpa using (hs.comm i j hij hj).symm

theorem unop_mem_adjoin {S : Set ℕ} {x : Aᵐᵒᵖ}
    (hx : x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R ((fun i => op (s i)) '' S)) :
    unop x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' S) := by
  induction hx using Algebra.adjoin_induction with
  | mem x hx =>
    obtain ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩ := hx
    exact Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩
  | algebraMap r =>
    simp [MulOpposite.algebraMap_apply]
  | add x y _ _ hx hy => simpa using add_mem hx hy
  | mul x y _ _ hx hy => simpa using mul_mem hy hx

/-- A trace on `A`, read on `Aᵐᵒᵖ`. -/
noncomputable def opTrace (t : A →ₗ[R] R) : Aᵐᵒᵖ →ₗ[R] R :=
  t ∘ₗ (MulOpposite.opLinearEquiv R (M := A)).symm.toLinearMap

@[simp] theorem opTrace_apply (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (x : Aᵐᵒᵖ) : opTrace t x = t (unop x) := rfl

end Opposite

/-! ### T: the Markov trace is invariant under word reversal, for every `N` -/

section Reversal

variable {A : Type*} [Ring A] [Algebra R A] {s : ℕ → A}

/-- **T for the Markov trace.** A normalised, cyclic Markov trace takes the same value on a
signed braid word and on its reversal. Every `N`, every ring, every quadratic, every letter
convention. -/
theorem markov_reverse (hs : HeckeFamily N a b s) (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1)
    (hcyc : ∀ x y : A, t (x * y) = t (y * x))
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    (α β : Bool → R) (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    t (evalWord α β s w.reverse) = t (evalWord α β s w) := by
  have hop := heckeFamily_op hs
  have h := markov_transfer hs hop z t ht1 hcyc hmark (opTrace t)
    (by simp [ht1]) (by intro x y; simp [hcyc])
    (by
      intro k hk x hx
      simp only [opTrace_apply, unop_mul, unop_op]
      rw [hcyc]
      exact hmark k hk _ (unop_mem_adjoin hx))
    (evalWord α β (pgen N a b) w)
  rw [map_evalWord (lift hs) α β _ s (fun i hi => lift_pgen hs hi),
    map_evalWord (lift hop) α β _ _ (fun i hi => lift_pgen hop hi), evalWord_op,
    opTrace_apply, unop_op] at h
  exact h.symm

end Reversal

/-! ### P: the mirror at fixed `q` is not a symmetry; it changes `(a, z)` to `(−a, z − a)` -/

section Mirror

variable {A : Type*} [Ring A] [Algebra R A] {s : ℕ → A}

/-- The Markov trace of a single generator is `z`. -/
theorem markov_gen (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1) (hN : 0 < N)
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x) :
    t (s 0) = z := by
  have := hmark 0 hN 1 (Subalgebra.one_mem _)
  rwa [one_mul, ht1, mul_one] at this

/-- **P with `q` fixed is not trace-preserving.** For `a ≠ 0`, the one-letter braid `σ₀`
and its mirror `σ₀⁻¹` have Markov traces `z` and `z − a`. -/
theorem mirror_not_trace_preserving (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1) (hN : 0 < N)
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    (ha : a ≠ 0) :
    ¬ ∀ w : List (Fin N × Bool),
        t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (mirror w)) = t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s w) := by
  intro h
  have hw := h [(⟨0, hN⟩, true)]
  have hg := markov_gen z t ht1 hN hmark
  simp [mirror, letter, stdα, stdβ, ht1, hg] at hw
  exact ha hw

/-- The two values in the refutation, explicitly: `tr_M(σ₀⁻¹) = tr_M(σ₀) − a`. -/
theorem trace_sigma0_inv (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1) (hN : 0 < N)
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x) :
    t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s [(⟨0, hN⟩, true)]) = z ∧
      t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s [(⟨0, hN⟩, false)]) = z - a := by
  have hg := markov_gen z t ht1 hN hmark
  constructor
  · simp [letter, stdα, stdβ, hg]
  · simp [letter, stdα, stdβ, ht1, hg]; ring

/-- **CPT, in the stated form, is not trace-preserving.** `C` does not act on Hecke
generators, so `C·P·T` on a word is `mirror ∘ reverse`; the witness is again `σ₀`. -/
theorem cpt_not_trace_preserving (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1) (hN : 0 < N)
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    (ha : a ≠ 0) :
    ¬ ∀ w : List (Fin N × Bool),
        t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (cpt w)) = t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s w) := by
  intro h
  have hc := h [(⟨0, hN⟩, true)]
  have hg := markov_gen z t ht1 hN hmark
  simp [cpt, mirror, letter, stdα, stdβ, ht1, hg] at hc
  exact ha hc

/-- The inverse generators `sᵢ⁻¹ = sᵢ − a` (case `b = 1`). -/
def invFamily (a : R) (s : ℕ → A) : ℕ → A := fun i => s i - a • (1 : A)

theorem mul_inv_gen (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) {i : ℕ} (hi : i < N) :
    s i * invFamily a s i = 1 := by
  simp only [invFamily, mul_sub, mul_smul_comm, mul_one, hs.quad i hi, one_smul]
  abel

theorem inv_mul_gen (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) {i : ℕ} (hi : i < N) :
    invFamily a s i * s i = 1 := by
  simp only [invFamily, sub_mul, smul_mul_assoc, one_mul, hs.quad i hi, one_smul]
  abel

/-- **The mirror family.** The inverses `sᵢ − a` satisfy the Hecke relations with quadratic
`x² = −a·x + 1`. -/
theorem heckeFamily_inv (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) : HeckeFamily N (-a) 1 (invFamily a s) where
  quad i hi := by
    simp only [invFamily, sub_mul, mul_sub, smul_mul_assoc, mul_smul_comm, one_mul, mul_one,
      hs.quad i hi, smul_sub, smul_smul, one_smul, neg_smul, neg_mul]
    abel
  braid i hi := by
    -- `u = s'ᵢ s'ⱼ s'ᵢ` is a left inverse and `w = s'ⱼ s'ᵢ s'ⱼ` a right inverse of
    -- `v = sᵢ sⱼ sᵢ = sⱼ sᵢ sⱼ`, so `u = w`.
    set x := s i
    set y := s (i + 1)
    set x' := invFamily a s i
    set y' := invFamily a s (i + 1)
    have hxx : x' * x = 1 := inv_mul_gen hs (by omega)
    have hyy : y' * y = 1 := inv_mul_gen hs hi
    have hxx' : x * x' = 1 := mul_inv_gen hs (by omega)
    have hyy' : y * y' = 1 := mul_inv_gen hs hi
    have hv : x * y * x = y * x * y := hs.braid i hi
    have hu : x' * y' * x' * (x * y * x) = 1 := by
      calc x' * y' * x' * (x * y * x) = x' * (y' * ((x' * x) * y) * x) := by simp only [mul_assoc]
        _ = 1 := by rw [hxx, one_mul, hyy, one_mul, hxx]
    have hw : (y * x * y) * (y' * x' * y') = 1 := by
      calc (y * x * y) * (y' * x' * y') = y * (x * ((y * y') * x') * y') := by simp only [mul_assoc]
        _ = 1 := by rw [hyy', one_mul, hxx', one_mul, hyy']
    calc x' * y' * x' = x' * y' * x' * ((y * x * y) * (y' * x' * y')) := by rw [hw, mul_one]
      _ = (x' * y' * x' * (x * y * x)) * (y' * x' * y') := by rw [← hv]; simp only [mul_assoc]
      _ = y' * x' * y' := by rw [hu, one_mul]
  comm i j hij hj := by
    have h := hs.comm i j hij hj
    simp only [invFamily, sub_mul, mul_sub, smul_mul_assoc, mul_smul_comm, one_mul, mul_one, h,
      smul_sub]
    abel

theorem invFamily_mem_adjoin {S : Set ℕ} {i : ℕ} (hi : i ∈ S) :
    invFamily a s i ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' S) :=
  sub_mem (Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩)
    (by rw [Algebra.smul_def, mul_one]; exact Subalgebra.algebraMap_mem _ a)

theorem adjoin_inv_le (S : Set ℕ) :
    Algebra.adjoin R (invFamily a s '' S) ≤ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' S) :=
  Algebra.adjoin_le (by rintro _ ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩; exact invFamily_mem_adjoin hi)

/-- **The Markov trace is a Markov trace for the mirror family, with parameter `z − a`.** -/
theorem markov_inv (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R)
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x) :
    ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (invFamily a s '' Set.Iio k),
      t (x * invFamily a s k) = (z - a) * t x := by
  intro k hk x hx
  have hx' := adjoin_inv_le (a := a) (s := s) (Set.Iio k) hx
  simp only [invFamily, mul_sub, mul_smul_comm, mul_one, map_sub, map_smul, smul_eq_mul,
    hmark k hk x hx']
  ring

/-- Evaluating the mirror of `w` with data `a` is evaluating `w` in the mirror family with
data `−a`. -/
theorem evalWord_mirror (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (mirror w) = evalWord (stdα (-a)) stdβ (invFamily a s) w := by
  induction w with
  | nil => rfl
  | cons p w ih =>
    simp only [mirror, List.map_cons] at ih ⊢
    rw [evalWord_cons, evalWord_cons, ih]
    congr 1
    obtain ⟨i, e⟩ := p
    cases e
    · simp [letter, stdα, stdβ, invFamily]
    · simp [letter, stdα, stdβ, invFamily]
      abel

/-- **Mirror law.** The mirror carries the Markov trace with data `(a, z)` to the Markov
trace with data `(−a, z − a)`: for any algebra `B` with a Hecke family `s'` of quadratic
`x² = −a·x + 1` and any normalised cyclic Markov trace `t'` on it with parameter `z − a`,
`t (eval_s (mirror w)) = t' (eval_{s'} w)`. -/
theorem mirror_law (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1)
    (hcyc : ∀ x y : A, t (x * y) = t (y * x))
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    {B : Type*} [Ring B] [Algebra R B] {s' : ℕ → B} (hs' : HeckeFamily N (-a) 1 s')
    (t' : B →ₗ[R] R) (ht1' : t' 1 = 1) (hcyc' : ∀ x y : B, t' (x * y) = t' (y * x))
    (hmark' : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s' '' Set.Iio k),
      t' (x * s' k) = (z - a) * t' x)
    (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (mirror w)) = t' (evalWord (stdα (-a)) stdβ s' w) := by
  have hinv := heckeFamily_inv hs
  have h := markov_transfer hinv hs' (z - a) t ht1 hcyc (markov_inv z t hmark)
    t' ht1' hcyc' hmark' (evalWord (stdα (-a)) stdβ (pgen N (-a) 1) w)
  rw [map_evalWord (lift hinv) _ _ _ _ (fun i hi => lift_pgen hinv hi),
    map_evalWord (lift hs') _ _ _ _ (fun i hi => lift_pgen hs' hi)] at h
  rw [evalWord_mirror]
  exact h

/-- **qCPT law.** `C·P·T` preserves the Markov trace once the parameters move with it:
the trace with data `(a, z)` of `cpt w` equals the trace with data `(−a, z − a)` of `w`, for
any normalised cyclic Markov trace `t'` with those data, in any algebra. With `a = q − q⁻¹`
this is `q ↦ q⁻¹`, `z ↦ z − (q − q⁻¹)`. It combines `mirror_law` with `markov_reverse`. -/
theorem qcpt_law (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R) (ht1 : t 1 = 1)
    (hcyc : ∀ x y : A, t (x * y) = t (y * x))
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    {B : Type*} [Ring B] [Algebra R B] {s' : ℕ → B} (hs' : HeckeFamily N (-a) 1 s')
    (t' : B →ₗ[R] R) (ht1' : t' 1 = 1) (hcyc' : ∀ x y : B, t' (x * y) = t' (y * x))
    (hmark' : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s' '' Set.Iio k),
      t' (x * s' k) = (z - a) * t' x)
    (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (cpt w)) = t' (evalWord (stdα (-a)) stdβ s' w) := by
  rw [cpt, mirror_law hs z t ht1 hcyc hmark hs' t' ht1' hcyc' hmark' w.reverse]
  exact markov_reverse hs' (z - a) t' ht1' hcyc' hmark' _ _ w

/-! ### Writhe normalisation absorbs the `z` shift -/

/-- The writhe of a signed word: `+1` per positive letter, `−1` per negative one. -/
def writhe : List (Fin N × Bool) → ℤ
  | [] => 0
  | p :: w => (if p.2 then 1 else -1) + writhe w

theorem writhe_mirror (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) : writhe (mirror w) = -writhe w := by
  induction w with
  | nil => rfl
  | cons p w ih =>
    simp only [mirror, List.map_cons] at ih ⊢
    rw [writhe, writhe, ih]
    cases p.2 <;> simp <;> ring

theorem writhe_append (u v : List (Fin N × Bool)) : writhe (u ++ v) = writhe u + writhe v := by
  induction u with
  | nil => simp [writhe]
  | cons p u ih => simp only [List.cons_append, writhe, ih]; ring

theorem writhe_reverse (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) : writhe w.reverse = writhe w := by
  induction w with
  | nil => rfl
  | cons p w ih => rw [List.reverse_cons, writhe_append, ih, writhe, writhe, writhe]; ring

theorem writhe_cpt (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) : writhe (cpt w) = -writhe w := by
  rw [cpt, writhe_mirror, writhe_reverse]

/-- **Normalisation data.** A unit `μ` with `μ² z = z − a`. Then `D = μ z` is the loop value,
and `μ^{writhe} · tr` (times `D^{-(n-1)}`) is the writhe-normalised (HOMFLY-type) invariant:
`μ z = D` makes a positive Markov move free, and `μ⁻¹ (z − a) = μ z = D` a negative one. -/
def IsNormaliser (a z : R) (μ : Rˣ) : Prop := (μ : R) * μ * z = z - a

/-- **The mirror data.** If `μ` normalises `(a, z)` then `μ⁻¹` normalises the mirror data
`(−a, z − a)`, and the loop value is unchanged: `μ⁻¹ (z − a) = μ z`. So in the normalised
invariant the mirror is `(a, μ) ↦ (−a, μ⁻¹)` with `D` fixed; `z` no longer moves on its own. -/
theorem isNormaliser_inv {a z : R} {μ : Rˣ} (h : IsNormaliser a z μ) :
    IsNormaliser (-a) (z - a) μ⁻¹ ∧ ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (z - a) = μ * z := by
  have hu : ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * μ = 1 := by simp
  have key : ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (z - a) = μ * z := by
    rw [← h]
    calc ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (μ * μ * z) = (((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * μ) * (μ * z) := by ring
      _ = μ * z := by rw [hu, one_mul]
  refine ⟨?_, key⟩
  unfold IsNormaliser
  calc ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (z - a)
      = ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (z - a)) := by ring
    _ = ((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * (μ * z) := by rw [key]
    _ = (((μ⁻¹ : Rˣ) : R) * μ) * z := by ring
    _ = z - a - -a := by rw [hu, one_mul]; ring

/-- **Normalised mirror law.** With `μ` a normaliser for `(a, z)`:
`μ^{e(mirror w)} · tr_{a,z}(mirror w) = (μ⁻¹)^{e(w)} · tr_{−a,z−a}(w)`, and `μ⁻¹` is the
normaliser for `(−a, z − a)` with the same loop value (`isNormaliser_inv`). The `z` shift is
absorbed: the writhe-normalised invariant sees only `(a, μ) ↦ (−a, μ⁻¹)`. -/
theorem normalised_mirror_law (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R)
    (ht1 : t 1 = 1) (hcyc : ∀ x y : A, t (x * y) = t (y * x))
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    {B : Type*} [Ring B] [Algebra R B] {s' : ℕ → B} (hs' : HeckeFamily N (-a) 1 s')
    (t' : B →ₗ[R] R) (ht1' : t' 1 = 1) (hcyc' : ∀ x y : B, t' (x * y) = t' (y * x))
    (hmark' : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s' '' Set.Iio k),
      t' (x * s' k) = (z - a) * t' x)
    (μ : Rˣ) (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    ((μ ^ writhe (mirror w) : Rˣ) : R) * t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (mirror w))
      = ((μ⁻¹ ^ writhe w : Rˣ) : R) * t' (evalWord (stdα (-a)) stdβ s' w) := by
  rw [mirror_law hs z t ht1 hcyc hmark hs' t' ht1' hcyc' hmark' w, writhe_mirror, zpow_neg,
    inv_zpow]

/-- **Normalised qCPT law.** Same statement for `C·P·T`: the writhe-normalised invariant at
`(a, μ)` of `cpt w` equals the one at `(−a, μ⁻¹)` of `w`. -/
theorem normalised_qcpt_law (hs : HeckeFamily N a 1 s) (z : R) (t : A →ₗ[R] R)
    (ht1 : t 1 = 1) (hcyc : ∀ x y : A, t (x * y) = t (y * x))
    (hmark : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s '' Set.Iio k), t (x * s k) = z * t x)
    {B : Type*} [Ring B] [Algebra R B] {s' : ℕ → B} (hs' : HeckeFamily N (-a) 1 s')
    (t' : B →ₗ[R] R) (ht1' : t' 1 = 1) (hcyc' : ∀ x y : B, t' (x * y) = t' (y * x))
    (hmark' : ∀ k, k < N → ∀ x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s' '' Set.Iio k),
      t' (x * s' k) = (z - a) * t' x)
    (μ : Rˣ) (w : List (Fin N × Bool)) :
    ((μ ^ writhe (cpt w) : Rˣ) : R) * t (evalWord (stdα a) stdβ s (cpt w))
      = ((μ⁻¹ ^ writhe w : Rˣ) : R) * t' (evalWord (stdα (-a)) stdβ s' w) := by
  rw [qcpt_law hs z t ht1 hcyc hmark hs' t' ht1' hcyc' hmark' w, writhe_cpt, zpow_neg,
    inv_zpow]

end Mirror

end QOU.IwahoriHecke.MarkovTraceSymmetries
